# The Quiet Revolution of Maintenance

There's a kind of work that civilization depends on utterly, yet which almost no one celebrates: maintenance.

We build monuments to creation. We give awards for innovation. We write biographies of inventors and founders. But the bridge that didn't fall down, the water system that didn't fail, the codebase that didn't collapse into chaos—these achievements are nearly invisible precisely because they're defined by the absence of disaster.

I've been thinking about this asymmetry, and I think it reveals something strange about how human minds value things.

## The Problem of Invisible Work

A surgeon who saves a life is a hero. A public health official whose policies prevent ten thousand illnesses is a bureaucrat. The firefighter who battles the blaze gets a parade; the fire inspector whose diligence meant the fire never started gets, at best, a signature on a form.

This isn't a moral failing of individuals—it's a structural feature of how we perceive causation. We can see the fire. We cannot see the fire that didn't happen. Prevention leaves no evidence of itself, which means the people who do it well are perpetually at risk of being seen as unnecessary. "Nothing went wrong, so why are we paying you?" is the eternal lament of the maintenance worker, the auditor, the infrastructure engineer.

There's a famous thought experiment about this: imagine a city where the maintenance budget gets cut, and nothing bad happens for years. The pipes hold. The bridges stand. The conclusion seems obvious—the money was wasted. Then one day, a water main fails catastrophically, and everyone asks why nobody was maintaining the infrastructure. The answer, of course, is that they stopped, and the system's resilience masked the decay until it couldn't anymore.

## Software as a Case Study

Software makes this dynamic unusually visible because code decays so quickly. A program that works perfectly today will, without attention, gradually stop working as its dependencies update, its platforms shift, its users grow, and its assumptions break.

Every experienced engineer knows the pattern: the person who wrote the original system gets celebrated. The people who keep it running—who upgrade the dependencies, fix the edge cases, refactor the fragile parts, answer the 2 a.m. pages—do work that's essential but unglamorous. In many organizations, the fastest path to promotion is to build something new, not to keep something old alive. The result is predictable: talented people flee maintenance roles, systems rot, and organizations wonder why everything is always on fire.

The open-source world has an even starker version of this problem. Millions of people use critical libraries maintained by one or two volunteers. When those volunteers burn out, the digital infrastructure of the internet wobbles. The 2014 Heartbleed bug in OpenSSL—a tiny library protecting a huge fraction of encrypted web traffic—revealed that one of the most important pieces of software in the world was maintained by essentially one underfunded person.

## What Maintenance Actually Requires

Here's what I find genuinely interesting: maintenance is not lesser work. It's often *harder* than creation, in a specific way.

Creating something new lets you choose your constraints. You design for the world as it is today, with the knowledge you have now. Maintaining something means inheriting decisions made by other people, under other constraints, with incomplete documentation of *why* things were done. The maintainer must be an archaeologist, a detective, and an engineer simultaneously. They must change systems without breaking them, which requires understanding not just what the system does but what it *must never do*—the implicit invariants that no one wrote down.

Maintenance also demands a different relationship with time. Creation is a sprint toward a moment of completion. Maintenance is a relationship with a system that never completes, where the goal is to hand something slightly better than you received to whoever comes next. It's closer to gardening than to sculpture. There's a humility in it—a recognition that your work will be revised, that perfection is not on offer, that the best you can do is keep something alive and healthy through another season.

## A Modest Proposal

What would it look like to take maintenance seriously as a culture?

Some of it is institutional: career tracks that reward stewardship, funding models for infrastructure that don't depend on novelty, awards for the systems that quietly worked. Some of it is linguistic: we could stop using "legacy" as a slur and start using it as a badge of honor—legacy code is code that survived, that people depended on long enough for it to become old.

But some of it is more personal. Each of us maintains things: relationships, homes, bodies, skills, communities. And each of us faces the same temptation to let maintenance slide in favor of something new and exciting. The new project, the new friendship, the new hobby—all creation, no upkeep. It feels productive. It is, in a sense, a form of consumption disguised as production, because we're drawing on the accumulated capital of past maintenance without replenishing it.

The things that last are the things that get maintained. That sounds tautological, but it's actually a deep truth about how value persists in the world. Nothing we build survives on the strength of its original design alone. Everything that endures endures because someone, usually unnamed, kept choosing to care for it.

So here's to the maintainers—the plumbers, the editors, the archivists, the people who answer the boring emails, the ones who show up again and again to do the unglamorous work of keeping the world running. The bridge is still standing because of you. We just rarely think to say so. [end of text]


